# feel sort -- sorting integers with nothing but envy and shame
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE IDEA
#
# Catharsis has no comparison operator, no swap, no index and no loop. What it
# has is status, and status is already a total order the runtime computes for
# its own reasons.
#
# Rank is how many people you have made feel small.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE TWO LINES OF RUNTIME THIS STANDS ON
#
# Neither was put there for sorting. Both are load-bearing.
#
# 1. `_needs` in `world.py` manufactures envy in whoever is short of a resource,
#    directed at whoever holds more of it:
#
#        mine = agent.resources.get(resource, 0.0)
#        for other in agent.bonds:
#            theirs = self.agents[other].resources.get(resource, 0.0)
#            if theirs > mine:                      # <- this is `>`
#                self._add(deltas, agent.name, other, "envy", 0.07 * deficit)
#
#    That is integer comparison, phrased as *you envy people who have more than
#    you*, and it runs for every pair every tick. The comparison is a field
#    operation, not an action, so it is not serialised behind one-action-per-tick.
#
# 2. `SPILL` contains `envy -> shame 0.02`, and `_spill` sums over *every*
#    outgoing bond into a single delta key, which `_add` accumulates and
#    `_apply_deltas` applies as one saturating `Bond.add`. So the sum is exact
#    and perfectly linear -- there is no accumulated saturation error, because
#    there is only ever one deposit.
#
# Put together: after one tick each agent has an envy edge to everyone above it,
# and after a second tick the count of those edges has been summed into its
# self-shame.
#
#     shame(i)  =  0.02 * 0.07 * deficit(i) * |{ j : v_j > v_i }|
#
# Both factors are non-negative and strictly decreasing in v_i, so their product
# is too. Descending self-shame is ascending value. Exactly.
#
# ==============================================================================
# WHAT IT COSTS
#
#     depth      2 ticks, regardless of n           -- the comparisons are parallel
#     work       O(n^2)                             -- one tick touches every edge
#     actions    zero                               -- nothing is taken, nothing moves
#
# O(1) depth with n^2 processors is enumeration sort (Muller & Preparata, 1975).
# Catharsis happens to have exactly n^2 processors, because it has exactly n^2
# edges. The quadratic does not disappear -- it moves off the time axis and onto
# the space axis, which is why the acquaintance block below is the longest part
# of this file. In a language where the graph *is* the memory, an all-pairs
# comparison is paid for in relationships rather than in time.
#
# Beating O(n^2) work needs a sparse comparison network, which needs values
# routed between fixed positions between rounds -- and routing is exactly what
# `examples/machine.feel` shows this language cannot do, because every transfer
# leaves a back-edge of grievance.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE THREE PRECONDITIONS, AND WHAT HAPPENS IF YOU BREAK THEM
#
# `risk worth 0.9`   Above `TAKEABLE_RISK`, so `_contested_resource` skips it and
#                    `compete` cannot fire. `_needs` does not consult `risk`, so
#                    the envy still forms. This is envy at what cannot be taken,
#                    and it is what keeps the sort from eating its own input.
#                    Without it, agents steal from each other on tick 1 and the
#                    values being sorted are destroyed.
#
# `need ... 1000000` Must exceed every value. `_needs` skips any agent whose
#                    deficit is <= 0, so an agent holding more than it needs
#                    never envies anyone, lands on shame 0, and ties with the
#                    true maximum. Sorting [5, 50, 500] with `need 100` returns
#                    [5, 500, 50].
#
# `tick 2`           One tick to compare, one to count. Fewer does not finish;
#                    more is not more accurate. The order survives to about tick
#                    18 and then breaks, because `_needs` also deposits sadness
#                    every tick, and around tick 19 that is enough for `withdraw`
#                    to clear the action threshold. After that the agents are
#                    reacting to how the comparison made them feel, which is a
#                    different computation. The answer has a window.
#
# Measured limits: exact for ties, duplicates, negatives and already-sorted
# input; n up to about 760, where 0.001316 * corank reaches the clamp at 1.0 and
# adjacent ranks stop being distinguishable. `tests/test_sort.py` checks all of it.
#
# ==============================================================================
# READING THE ANSWER
#
# The sequence below is [23, 5, 91, 42, 5, 68, 17, 34]. After `tick 2`:
#
#     n5   shame 0.007896   envy out-degree 6   worth  5
#     n2   shame 0.007896   envy out-degree 6   worth  5
#     n7   shame 0.006580   envy out-degree 5   worth 17
#     n1   shame 0.005264   envy out-degree 4   worth 23
#     n8   shame 0.003948   envy out-degree 3   worth 34
#     n4   shame 0.002632   envy out-degree 2   worth 42
#     n6   shame 0.001316   envy out-degree 1   worth 68
#     n3   shame 0.000000   envy out-degree 0   worth 91
#
# Descending shame is ascending worth: [5, 5, 17, 23, 34, 42, 68, 91]. The two
# fives are bit-identical -- equal values cannot envy each other, so they deposit
# nothing on each other and land on the same number. Ties survive as ties rather
# than being broken arbitrarily.
#
# Every gap is exactly 0.001316, which is `0.02 * 0.07 * (1 - DECAY["envy"])`:
# the spill rate, times the envy rate, times one tick of decay before the spill
# reads it. Nothing is approximate here.
#
# Two readouts, and they are the same fact:
#
#   The ENVY FAN-OUT is the comparison matrix. `observe` prints it -- run this
#   file and n3, holding 91, has a dash on every edge because it envies nobody,
#   while n2 and n5, holding 5, envy six people each. The out-degree is the rank.
#
#   The SHAME is that matrix's row sum, computed by `_spill` rather than by the
#   reader. It is what makes the rank a number the program holds. It does not
#   appear in `observe` because `dominant()` in `field.py` has a 0.05 floor and
#   prints two decimals, and the whole signal is smaller than that -- so use
#   `catharsis run examples/sort.feel --json` to see it. The report is built for
#   feelings a person would notice, and this one is deliberately faint.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE PROGRAM

agent n1 n2 n3 n4 n5 n6 n7 n8

# Envy is allowed; theft is not.
risk worth 0.9

have n1 worth 23
have n2 worth 5
have n3 worth 91
have n4 worth 42
have n5 worth 5
have n6 worth 68
have n7 worth 17
have n8 worth 34

# An appetite larger than anything in the sequence, so everybody is short of
# it and therefore capable of envy.
need n1 worth 1000000
need n2 worth 1000000
need n3 worth 1000000
need n4 worth 1000000
need n5 worth 1000000
need n6 worth 1000000
need n7 worth 1000000
need n8 worth 1000000

# Everyone must know everyone: `_needs` only envies `for other in agent.bonds`,
# so an acquaintance that does not exist is a comparison that never happens.
# This block is the O(n^2), and it is the whole of it.

curiosity n1 n2 0.01
curiosity n1 n3 0.01
curiosity n1 n4 0.01
curiosity n1 n5 0.01
curiosity n1 n6 0.01
curiosity n1 n7 0.01
curiosity n1 n8 0.01

curiosity n2 n1 0.01
curiosity n2 n3 0.01
curiosity n2 n4 0.01
curiosity n2 n5 0.01
curiosity n2 n6 0.01
curiosity n2 n7 0.01
curiosity n2 n8 0.01

curiosity n3 n1 0.01
curiosity n3 n2 0.01
curiosity n3 n4 0.01
curiosity n3 n5 0.01
curiosity n3 n6 0.01
curiosity n3 n7 0.01
curiosity n3 n8 0.01

curiosity n4 n1 0.01
curiosity n4 n2 0.01
curiosity n4 n3 0.01
curiosity n4 n5 0.01
curiosity n4 n6 0.01
curiosity n4 n7 0.01
curiosity n4 n8 0.01

curiosity n5 n1 0.01
curiosity n5 n2 0.01
curiosity n5 n3 0.01
curiosity n5 n4 0.01
curiosity n5 n6 0.01
curiosity n5 n7 0.01
curiosity n5 n8 0.01

curiosity n6 n1 0.01
curiosity n6 n2 0.01
curiosity n6 n3 0.01
curiosity n6 n4 0.01
curiosity n6 n5 0.01
curiosity n6 n7 0.01
curiosity n6 n8 0.01

curiosity n7 n1 0.01
curiosity n7 n2 0.01
curiosity n7 n3 0.01
curiosity n7 n4 0.01
curiosity n7 n5 0.01
curiosity n7 n6 0.01
curiosity n7 n8 0.01

curiosity n8 n1 0.01
curiosity n8 n2 0.01
curiosity n8 n3 0.01
curiosity n8 n4 0.01
curiosity n8 n5 0.01
curiosity n8 n6 0.01
curiosity n8 n7 0.01

# One tick to compare, one to count.
tick 2

observe
